milarity to ecological knowledge.</article-title><source>Journal of Experimental Psychology: Learning, Memory, and Cognition</source><volume>29</volume><fpage>641</fpage><lpage>649</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005881-Johnson1"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>KE</given-names></name><name><surname>Mervis</surname><given-names>CB</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>Effects of varying levels of expertise on the basic level of categorization.</article-title><source>J Exp Psychol: Gen</source><volume>126</volume><fpage>248</fpage><lpage>277</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9281832</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005881-Lin1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>EL</given-names></name><name><surname>Murphy</surname><given-names>GL</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Thematic relations in adults' concepts.</article-title><source>Journal of Experimental Psychology: General</source><volume>130</volume><fpage>3</fpage><lpage>28</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11293459</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005881-Rabinowitz1"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rabinowitz</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hogan</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Using a Triad Judgment Task to Examine the Effect of Experience on Problem Representation in Statistics.</article-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Stenning</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><source>Proceedings of the Twenty-Third Annual Conference of the Cognitive Science Society</source><pu